Date: Sun, 28 Feb 93 11:05:50 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: BOMB: Will WTC Bombing Encourage Decentralization?

I'm surprised there hasn't been more discussion of this bombing, though I
assume the Extropian connections are not clear.

However, I assume Perry's outfit, Shearson Lehman, will be affected in some
way. 

There seem to be two main issues with connections to Extropianism:

1. Possibilities for new crackdowns on personal liberties, on dissemination
of information (on explosives, on Loompanics-style information services,
etc.), and on the various underground or marginal mailing lists.

2. The bombing of the World Trade Center may spur moves to further
decentralize markets and hubs of activity.

On this second point, recall that when the U.K. went to a distributed,
"NASDAQ"-like system (called the "Big Bang"), the optional floor-trading
system vanished almost immediately (that is, within weeks). I know there
are arguments about the NYSE-style of having floor traders, specialists,
etc., being more efficient in some sense than distributed, NASDAQ-style
markets. But the logistic and security advantages of fully-distributed
trading systems seem pretty compelling to me. Perhaps whatever makes the
NYSE-type system advantageous could be implemented in a computerized
trading system.

Personally, I find the whole idea of working in a 105-story antheap to be
pretty strange. 

-Tim May
