                 Copyright 1993 The Economist Newspaper, Ltd.

                             The Economist

                           February 20, 1993

SECTION: Business; Pg. 61 (U.K. Edition Pg. 81)

LENGTH: 1448 words

HEADLINE: Virtual reality gets real

DATELINE: SAN RAFAEL AND LEICESTER

HIGHLIGHT: One day virtual reality will revolutionise the
entertainment industry.  Right now, the technology is enjoying rather
more prosaic success

BODY:

A TATTOOED man in a black T-shirt stands in the middle of a
laboratory, wearing what looks like bulky, space-age flying goggles
and gesticulating with two hand-held electronic sensors.  Through a
microphone, he barks commands at a voice-activated computer.  Cinema
audiences, familiar with films like "Lawnmower Man", will recognise
the mad-scientist domain of virtual reality.  The technology uses
computer- generated, three-dimensional images to create the illusion
that its user is wandering through a real landscape.  But this is no
film.  The laboratory belongs to Autodesk, one of America's biggest
software producers.  The firm, based in Sausalito, California, had
sales of more than $ 300m last year and three-quarters of the world
market for computer-aided-design (CAD) software.  The tattooed man
could be designing anything from an office block to a jet engine.

Since the first virtual-reality firms set up shop in (inevitably)
California in the mid-1980s, two myths have grown up about the
industry.  The first is that, as with early artificial intelligence,
virtual reality is a clever idea with few serious applications beyond
"electronic LSD".  After all, Timothy Leary, a professor who in the
1960s advocated the use of that drug, regularly speaks at
virtual-reality conferences.  Second, most of the buzz about virtual
reality has revolved around its potential as dazzling entertainment.
The technology, runs the argument, is Hollywood-bound.

It may take some time to get there.  Several virtual-reality companies
already make a living in the entertainment industry, but only at the
lower reaches of the market.  Take W Industries, based in Leicester,
England.  Last year it sold around 300 of its Virtuality machines,
costing from L23,000 ($ 32,500) to L100,000 apiece, to amusement
arcades in 17 countries.  Despite their hazy images and boomy sounds,
the virtual worlds in which Virtuality players become immersed
(playing games such as "Dactyl Nightmare") can seem oddly realistic.

Although ever-faster computer chips are starting to make virtual
reality more lifelike, its images are still too fuzzy and slow-moving
for the popcorn-munching cinema-goer.  Virtual audio, which creates
the illusion of 3-D sound, is still too costly for the entertainment
market.  And virtual touch, which would allow users to "feel" objects,
has yet to make it out of research laboratories.  One Hollywood mogul
who has closely followed the industry reckons the widespread use of
virtual- reality technology in Tinseltown is "a decade away".

Technology is only half the problem.  What the entertainment side of
the virtual-reality industry really needs is a team of good
scriptwriters, to create virtual siblings of Nintendo's Super Mario
and Sega's Sonic the Hedgehog. Mattel's Power Glove, an early attempt
to bring virtual reality to video games, flopped partly because there
was no whizzy software for it.

Slowly, this creative gap is being filled.  Sphere, a computer-games
maker, has created a new division called CyberStudio, which produces
software for virtual-reality entertainment systems.  Electronic Arts,
another games maker, recently formed a joint venture -- known as 3DO
-- with Matsushita, Time Warner and AT&T.  It aims to design and build
virtual-reality systems and other interactive entertainment products.
And Virtual World Entertainment, which operates virtual-reality
entertainment centres in America and Japan, was recently bought by Tim
Disney, Walt's nephew.

 Less stimulation, more simulation

Although virtual reality may still lack the crispness and creativity
demanded by the entertainment industry, it offers business and
scientific users an immensely useful application: simulation.  Using
virtual-reality systems little more sophisticated than W Industries'
Virtuality machine, scientists can "walk around" models of complex
molecules to see what makes them tick.  Architects, too, can wander
through virtual office- blocks to check whether the corridors are too
narrow.  And "sitting" in a virtual car to see if the controls and
instruments are in the right place is cheaper than building lots of
costly prototypes.

Chrysler and IBM are already working together on virtual cars.  Boeing
is using virtual-reality models to design engineering-access tunnels
and interiors for its aeroplanes.  British Aerospace aims to use
virtual reality to display 3-D data for fighter-aircraft pilots.
Autodesk says one of its prospective customers is a shoe company that
wants to build simulated shops to see how different retailing designs
work.  And a virtual-reality system sold by Seattle-based Ixion allows
doctors to learn how to use endoscopes to carry out internal
examinations without experimenting on real patients.

Division, a virtual-reality firm based in Bristol, south-west England,
is working with the University of York, IBM and Glaxo, a big drugs
group, on software which, says Pierre duPont, Division's marketing
director, allows scientists to grab virtual models of molecules and
discover how they fit together.  Even W Industries, whose L6m annual
turnover is generated almost entirely from entertainment systems, sees
its future in simulation.  In five years' time, says John Tweedy, its
business- development director, half the firm's revenues will come
from virtual- reality applications for industry.

So far the organisation showing the most interest virtual-reality
products is the Pentagon.  The American armed forces talk of spending
$ 500m on virtual-reality simulators over the next four years.  Apart
from its relative insensitivity to cost, the Pentagon also has another
advantage: wall-to- wall supercomputers powerful enough to grind out
more-realistic virtual realities.

Most companies, however, want virtual-reality programs to run on
personal computers or workstations.  Virtual-reality firms are
tripping over each other to launch such products.  Autodesk this month
unveiled new software for developers to use to write CAD applications
for engineers, architects and industrial designers.  The firm's $
2,500 Cyberspace Developer Kit is being sold as a "complete toolset
for 3-D visualisation and simulation" for PCs.

Autodesk's San Rafael-based "Cyberspace" unit -- the word was coined
by a science-fiction author, William Gibson, in his 1984 novel
Neuromancer -- is now its fastest-growing division.  A software
"tool-kit" for PCs sold by Sense8, in Sausalito, allows users to
"walk" around everything from engineering prototypes to 3-D charts of
share prices.

Simply because of its size and existing CAD customer base, Autodesk is
the most significant entry into the virtual-reality market so far.  It
may not hold that position for long.  That is partly because big
American companies like Apple, IBM, Sun and Silicon Graphics are also
spending heavily on virtual-reality R&D.  But Japan's electronics and
computer giants are a more imminent threat.  Most industry-watchers
had expected Japanese interest in virtual reality to centre on the
technology's entertainment potential.  Only Sony, with hefty
investments in America's entertainment business, is taking that
approach.  The rest of Japan's giants are also plunging into
industrial and commercial virtual reality.

In a showroom in the Shinjuku district of Tokyo, Matsushita has
created a "virtual kitchen".  Customers can try the kitchen of their
dreams before it is built -- and as a result, reckons Matsushita, are
more likely to buy it.  Within a decade the firm hopes to have up to
100 virtual showrooms.  NEC has developed a system that uses virtual
ski-slopes to teach novices how to ski.  Both Toshiba and Fujitsu are
working on commercial virtual- reality systems; when they are
launched, probably in 1994, they are likely to be both cheaper and
more powerful than their American counterparts.

Fierce competition has already claimed the industry's first notable
victim -- VPL, a California-based firm whose founder, Jaron Lanier,
coined the term "virtual reality" and pioneered its early development.
Last December Thomson, a French electronics giant and one of VPL's
backers, seized the firm's patents -- and ousted Mr Lanier -- after
the company defaulted on loans.

Steve Ditlea, a New York-based analyst, is sanguine about VPL's
failure.  He compares the firm to MITS, which invented the
microcomputer in the 1970s.  MITS failed, but many of those involved
in the venture -- including Bill Gates, who wrote the firm's software
-- thrived afterwards.  The software skills of America's
virtual-reality pioneers may be their saving, too.  Which is why the
comeback to Mr Lanier, a talented programmer, is a virtual certainty.


