Date: Sun, 28 Feb 93 22:33:23 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

I've been running some simulations of our 'sucker bet', and can now explain
why the layer can always win under certain highly contrived conditions, and
yet the bettor will always win under normal conditions. 

Just to recap, the bet has a negative log return but a positive linear return.
The layer offers a 60% chance that your money will halve, and a 40% chance
that your money will double. It turns out that the only way the layer can
guarantee to win from this is to insist that the amount effectively staked on
each bet varies according to the sequence of results, AND that the bettor
leaves his money in for an extremely long series of bets.

What I found particularly interesting in this case was that for an infinitely
long series of bets, level stakes guarantees a profit for the bettor, and
lowering the stake after a loss and raising after a win guarantees the layer
to receive all the bettor's money. It is only the allocation of stakes that
is altered and yet this guarantees to turn the tables every time. 

To see why this is so, it's easier to consider an example where the odds are
longer and the winning rate lower. Let's make it an 11% chance that your
investment goes up tenfold, and an 89% chance that it is reduced by an order
of magnitude. (Odds offered are 10 / 1.) Firstly, if the bettor is allowed to
bet level stakes, he wins. Start with $1.00 and if a loss this loses $0.90.
If a win this wins $9.00. But following a loss you top up to the original
value, following a win you withdraw it leaving $1.00 each time. The amount
staked is $0.90 per round (the $0.10 is effectively just held in escrow by
the bookie) and the average profit to the bettor per round is $0.189. An
18.9% return if you count the full $1 or a 21% return on each 90 cent stake. 

But the log return is a huge loss! If the bettor isn't allowed to bet $0.90
level stakes on each round by topping up or withdrawing, his account soon
hits zero. The log return is - 83.4%. Although if operating to level stakes
the bettor would continue to take steady profits as long as he continued,
things are very different. Every time he loses (most rounds), he then has
to attempt to recover with a greatly reduced stake. Usually his stake has
been reduced by several orders of magnitude before he even manages to get a
winner, and if he does, the likelihood is that a few more losers will bring
the stake down to a tiny fraction of a cent. (Sums down to about 1 EE -38
of a dollar are allowable for purposes of calculation.) The result is that
the stakes invested on losers averages much greater than that invested on
winners, so the bettor is in extreme peril. 

If you consider the other extreme, odds of 10-1 on with 99% winners would
yield 8.9% to level stakes, but the log return is still positive so we 
cannot use this example.

However, being forced to use lower stakes after a loss and higher after a win
is not necessarily disastrous for the bettor. To return to our 60% chance of
halving and 40% chance of doubling, I found that he would tend to win after a
short series. After twenty rounds my simulations showed that the original sum
would on average be 3.5 to 4 x higher.

Another way that the bettor could win would be by being allowed to quit at
any time. Let's say he starts with $1,000 and chooses $16,000 as a target.
(Either hits the target and makes $15,000 that series, or crashes out with a
loss of $1,000.) I found that after 1,000 complete series the target was hit
on 190 occasions. So 190 x 15,000 - 810 x 1,000 = a gain of $2,040,000 from
$1,000,000 invested. With $2,000 as the target the $1 million only produced
$352,000. But with $1 million as the target this was regularly hit well
within the 1,001 goes necessary to break even. It took 32 series on my first
simulation, 233 on the next and two goes later the $1 million was taken after
just three $1,000 losses.

So the only way the layer can win is by absolving himself of any liability
during the period whenever the bettor's total is up, and possibly extremely
high. As well as having control of the bettor's staking allocation. The layer
has to wait for a very large number of bets, after which for a negative log
return the bettor's account will eventually hit zero. I don't think any
investor would take up the offer under these conditions.

I conclude that whilst log return may be of some academic interest, I think
linear return will be of more practical use to investors in the real world.

