Date: Sun, 28 Feb 93 15:14:16 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Cover's ideas

I don't completely follow Steve Whitrow's claims regarding how a
bet layer for a bet with a positive linear return can cause the
bettor to lose.  It sounds like Steve is just saying that the layer
can wait for the bettor to get behind and then stop the series.  Due
to the "random walk" nature of the bet, all possible positions will
be visited, so this is guaranteed to happen eventually.  I'm not sure,
but I'd guess that this would be the case for any bet which can go
up or down.  Maybe someone who knows how random walks work could
discuss the circumstances under which all points are visited.  To
the extent that this is true of any betting situation, it doesn't
really shed much light on this case.

In any case, the average return for the bet that we are discussing is,
as I would figure it, about 10% of the amount invested.  The longer
you bet, the farther ahead the bettor will be on the average.  Long
series of bets therefore favor the bettor.  The amount he will be
ahead will be his average bet size, times 10%, times the number of
rounds.  If the layer can force the player to make small bets, he
can slow down the rate at which the bettor gains, but that's all.

Hal

