Several years ago I earned a few hundred bucks by doing some quick analyses
of a "sure thing" betting scheme that a couple I knew had come up with.
They were testing their "system" every weekend at Lake Tahoe, but wanted
some external confirmation from a "scientist" (me) that their scheme was
sound. I averred that I was neither a statistician nor a professional
gambler, but they persisted and practically begged me to do some consulting
for them. I agreed. (And they got a good deal, because I only charged them
for a couple of hours but I actually spent most of the evening before
brushing up on the "theory of martingales" and other such gambling-related
stuff.)

Their system was deceptively (and naively) simple: recover a series of
losses by increasing the bet after each loss and quitting the game as soon
as they'd doubled their money (this quitting idea was their particular
brainstorm, as it were).

For example, start with a $100 bet. Lose it, so bet $200. Lose it, so bet
$400. Lose it, so bet $800. WIN!  The $800 winnings covers the $100 + $200
+ $400 = $700 already lost. Quit the game. Net profit: $100. Foolproof, eh?

(Why they needed me to confirm their "system," I'll never know. After I
pointed out the flaws, they coolly and politely peeled off the hundred
dollar bills for my consulting fee and departed. I haven't talked to them
since. Who knows, maybe they made a million and retired to the Cayman
Islands. I wouldn't bet on it, though.)

The flaws:

* these "log return" betting schemes are known as "martingales," and are
familiar to all gambling theorists. (BTW, our information theory friend,
Tom Cover, mentions them in passing but does not explore them in detail.)
To quote a brief definition from James and James (Mathematics Dictionary):

"MARTINGALE, n. A stochastic sequence x1, x2,..., or its continuous
analogue, such that, given x1, x2, ..., xn, the conditional expected value
of xn+1 is equal to xn. In particular, a system of betting planned so that
in a sequence of bets losses are recovered by progressively increasing the
stakes (e.g., by doubling the stakes after each loss, but reducing to the
original amount after a win). This is not an effective system, since it is
to be expected that a sequence of successive losses will soon carry the
stake beyond the player's resources or above the limit of the game."

* This is one reason casinos have betting limits (there are other reasons,
such as difficulty of collecting debts, government rules, bad public
relations, lawsuits by disgruntled bettors, etc.). Also, house odds are
rarely close to being fair, a detail I'm ignoring (and this couple ignored
it, too!).

* "Gambler's ruin" occurs when a gambler does not have the resources to
match the house, or is too frightened to. (Recall the above example--it
took a bet of $800 to win a mere $100...assuming one quit the game at that
point! Had the player lost the example round, and then bet $1600 to win the
next round, the winning would _still_ only be $100 ($1600 - $800 - $400 -
$200 - $100). It doesn't take a Putnam Fellow to see the trend: the max
winnings are your original bet!!! So you're just as well off showing up at
the casino and betting precisely one time--win or lose, you walk away. 

(And while your winnings remain fixed at your original bet, say, $100, your
potential for being wiped out are frightening. Who wants to bet $32,000 for
the 50-50 chance of winning their original $100 back?)

* I asked this couple if they'd start with $100 and quit the game
completely if they won on their first bet (as their own scheme called for
them to do). "Of course not!" was their not unexpected reply. When they
began speaking glowingly about "runs of luck," I knew it was hopeless. I
gave them some Excel simulations I'd done--even though the theory was
enough for me--and wished them luck (so to speak).

And so ends this tale.

By the way, there are some interesting links with the mathematics of the
"numbers bet" I just posted a short time ago. 

-Tim


