From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: BET: Announcing a New Bet

Extropians like to bet, or at least like to talk about bets. So here's one.

For some collection of publicly-printed numbers (such as heights of
mountains, lengths of rivers, populations of cities, etc.), to be obtained
from almanacs, encyclopedias, etc. (so we agree on what they are), the bet
will go as follows:

- I will pay the stakes (what we agree on, say, one cent) on each number
that begins with a 1, a 2, a 3, or a 4. That is, on 4 of the numbers. (By
convention, no number starts with a zero.)

- someone accepting the bet will pay the same stakes on each of the numbers
that begins with a 5, a 6, a 7, an 8, or a 9. That is, on 5 of the numbers.

Here are some examples of the numbers I'm talking about:

* the populations of the cities of the world, down the list as far as the
list goes. Thus, the "100 largest cities" or the "1000 largest cities" are
both acceptable as inputs for the bet.

* the heights of the 100 (or 200, or 500, etc.) tallest mountains, measured
either in meters or in feet (or in any other measures, provided the table
has not already been looked at to figure out the most advantageous
measure).

* the weights of the largest ships in the world, perhaps from some nautical
almanac or the like.

* the numbers of pages in the books in any given library, or even the
Library of Congress. (To make this more manageable, this could be done if
we were to meet in person by walking into a bookstore and selecting several
shelves of books at random. Or a neutral third party could look at some of
his shelves.

* the total sizes of the postings to this list, as measured in lines,
bytes, whatever. Pick the last 1000 messages to go through this list and
measure their sizes.

So there's no confusion, let me elaborate on a few of the conditions:

* the tables of numbers must be randomly selected, so that they cannot be
selected to favor one side or another who has already counted them up (BTW,
allowing a bettor to "switch sides" when the other side has proposed the
set of numbers to be bet upon is a "cut-and-choose" solution to the problem
of the deck being stacked, as it were).

* For lengths of rivers or heights of mountains, either meters or feet may
be used, so long as the choice in consistent.

* Tables of random numbers are not kosher, for various reasons.

* Details of the stakes, judges, etc., will have to negotiated.

Any takers?

-Tim

